+      <p>Insertion into an AVL tree has two stages. First the key is inserted as in a plain binary search tree, at a leaf. Then the heights are updated on the path from the new leaf back to the root. The first node on that path whose balance factor is \(2\) or \(-2\) is the <em>lowest unbalanced node</em>. A <em>rotation</em> re-links a node and one of its children so that the child takes the parent's place; Proposition 3.1 shows that it keeps the search order. The <em>heavy child</em> of a node with balance factor \(2\) is its left child, and of a node with balance factor \(-2\) it is its right child. A child <em>leans</em> the way its own balance factor points: a balance factor of \(1\) leans left, \(-1\) leans right, and \(0\) leans neither. If the heavy child's balance factor is \(0\) or has the same sign as the unbalanced node's, one <em>single rotation</em> at the unbalanced node is made. If it has the opposite sign, a <em>double rotation</em> is made: first a rotation at the child, then a rotation at the node. Remark 3.2 says what is and is not proved about this repair.</p>
